412	 * Measured on #415's thread: `github.com/user-attachments/files/<id>/<name>` answers
413	 * 302 to a signed `objects.githubusercontent.com` URL whose `X-Amz-Expires` is 300
414	 * seconds. A tool that reported the `Location` and left the second call to its caller
415	 * would hand back an address that is dead by the time anyone uses it, so the hop
416	 * happens here — and the body written is the target's, which is also why
417	 * `content_type` is read off the final response.
418	 *
419	 * ## Why the ceiling is enforced on the bytes and not on the header
420	 *
421	 * `content-length` is checked first because it is free. It is not sufficient: it is the
422	 * ENCODED length — measured on #415's thread as 40 bytes declaring a 9-byte body — so
423	 * a header under the ceiling does not mean a body under it. The count is therefore
424	 * kept as the body streams, the read stops at the ceiling rather than after it, and
425	 * nothing is written when it is crossed: a truncated document at the destination is
426	 * worse than none, because the caller cannot tell it from a whole one.
